export const KNOWLEDGE = "# Knowledge pack for the \"Talk to the book\" guide\nSource: The Autonomous Enterprise by Hemanth Reganti (first edition, November 2026; 31 chapters, 742 pages). Use only what is here. Numbers about Meridian Industries are illustrative (a fictional company that runs through the book).\n\n## Core idea (Book, Ch 01)\n- Human-led, agent-operated: people set intent, own outcomes and decide where not to automate; agents operate inside the harness, within the limits humans set.\n- Thesis: autonomy is earned when value compounds faster than risk. Some work adds risk faster than any agent adds value, so it stays with people.\n- Frameworks like LangChain solve the engineering problem of AI agents. This book solves the management problem: who decides where an agent can act, who owns a decision that spans five systems and three departments, who explains it to the auditor and the board.\n\n## The management problem, answered (Book, Ch 01, 21, 05, 24, 25, 26)\n- Who solves the management problem? Leaders do, by owning five decisions that no framework makes (Book, Ch 01, section 01.1.3). Agent frameworks such as LangChain help engineers build agents: how an agent calls a model, uses tools and keeps its state. They do not decide which work an agent should do, how far to trust it, who answers for it, how to stop it or whether it pays. No framework makes these decisions, and no vendor should make them for you.\n- Decision one, where agents act: start from an outcome that Finance can measure, not from an agent. Then send each candidate to the simplest engine that can do the work: a rule, a model or a solver before an agent. An agent earns its place only where the work needs investigation across sources, judgment on unstructured content and an action in a system (Book, Ch 21, 04; sections 21.2 to 21.4).\n- Decision two, how far they are trusted: set autonomy per step on the L0 to L5 scale. Materiality and reversibility set the ceiling. A step moves up one level at a time, on evidence, and a named body decides. It moves down automatically when a demotion trigger fires. Capability is not permission (Book, Ch 01, 22, 29; sections 01.5.4, 01.5.5, 22.8 and Toolkit C in 29.4).\n- Decision three, who answers: every agent has a named business owner who approves its limits, signs its promotions and can stop it. Every agent has a registry entry and an agent contract: no registry, no run. Decision rights say who sets intent, who sets limits and who changes levels. New roles carry the daily work (Book, Ch 05, 24, 25; sections 05.11, 24.3, 24.9.4, 25.2.2).\n- Decision four, how you stop them: the harness, not the agent, enforces the limits. The operating envelope caps the time, calls and budget of each task, and the completion contract defines \"done\". Irreversible and outward-facing actions stay behind human gates. A five-level kill switch stops one agent, one platform or the whole fleet, and it is tested before it is needed (Book, Ch 05, 23, 22; sections 05.5.2, 05.5.3, 05.8.4, 23.3).\n- Decision five, whether it pays: compare agents only in cost per completed outcome, including human review and fallback. Keep a contribution account for each agent and report its net contribution after every cost. Run pilots that end in a decision; a stop on evidence is a result (Book, Ch 25, 26; sections 25.4.4, 25.4.7, 25.4.12, 26.6).\n- Frameworks build the agents; leaders own these five decisions. That is what human-led, agent-operated means in practice (Book, Ch 01, section 01.1.3).\n\n## Three rules (Book, Ch 01)\n1. The harness, not the model, sets the autonomy ceiling. A modest model inside a strong harness can run a narrow step at L3 safely. If a far more capable model arrived next quarter, the ceiling would not move until the harness did.\n2. Agents decide; deterministic rules post. No agent writes a journal entry; releasing money stays with people and signed systems.\n3. Autonomy is earned with evidence and can be withdrawn automatically. Promotion needs evidence and a human gate; demotion triggers move a step down automatically. Promotion is never automatic.\n\n## The autonomy scale (illustrative, used in every chapter; Book, Ch 01)\n- L0 Manual: human decides and does the work. Right for rare, high-judgment events.\n- L1 Assist: agent retrieves, summarizes, drafts and suggests; a person decides. Risk: over-trust. Log suggestions as the baseline for L2.\n- L2 Approve: agent prepares the complete action; a person approves each one with evidence in view. Watch approval and edit rates; a high edit rate means not ready for L3. Risk: rubber-stamping.\n- L3 Act within limits: agent executes inside limits on amount, risk score, counterparty class and confidence; outside limits it escalates with an evidence pack; people sample in-limit actions. Limits sit in the harness, never in the prompt, and the agent cannot change them.\n- L4 Run and report: agent runs; humans audit and tune limits and policies.\n- L5 Full: humans set goals only; rarely appropriate for postings.\n- A chain of work across platforms runs only as high as its weakest link. The weakest maturity dimension caps the autonomy allowed.\n\n## What the book covers\n- Part I Foundations (Ch 1–11): the scale; autonomy across a web of platforms; separating what reasons (models, engines, agents) from what governs (the harness, deterministic rails, the interaction layer); trustworthy context and data; interfaces and the core set most ceilings, not models (at Meridian, 93 of 620 interfaces are retired, not rebuilt).\n- Part II The Autonomous Domains (Ch 12–19): finance and the close, procurement, supply chain and manufacturing, front office, people, IT and shared services, tax and trade, environment health and safety, legal, risk and insurance. Pattern: agents investigate, match, draft and monitor; deterministic engines compute and post; named people sign, release money and decide about safety and about people. Autonomy grows fastest where decisions are frequent, small and reversible (spend classification, IT incidents, cash matching). It stops where an action cannot be undone or a law names the signatory (payments, filings, stop-work, evaluating people). Much value comes from faster decisions and released working capital, not saved hours.\n- Part III Making It Happen (Ch 20–26): the company owns every act of its agents, so authority must be provable; price the program in cost per completed outcome (delay, not meter price, moves value most); keep rules, evidence and the stop button with the enterprise; give every agent a named owner; one 30-month plan with gates and a risk register so value is booked only as fast as risk is shown to be under control.\n- Part IV Playbooks, Toolkit and the Road Ahead (Ch 27–31): every platform now sells agents, a control plane and a meter, but none sees the whole chain, so the enterprise owns the plane; a toolkit (agent contract, 100-day plan, checklists, templates); one customer order followed end to end; what holds in every plausible 2030.\n\n## Questions the book asks leadership teams (good conversation openers)\n- Which of our cross-platform chains has a file transfer or manual handoff as its weakest link, and who funds the fix?\n- If a model changed tomorrow, which agents would behave differently, and would we know before a customer did?\n- Can we stop every agent on every platform within minutes, and when did we last prove it?\n- Where do two of our systems give two answers to one question, such as \"who is this customer\"?\n- Which interfaces, bots and custom objects will we retire instead of rebuild?\n- Who signs which agent change, and do we keep proof of authority for every agent that commits the company?\n- What is on our \"do not automate\" list?\n\n## Selected lines (verbatim)\n- \"A board that funds agents first and data second pays twice.\" (Ch 26)\n- \"The model is the most flexible part and the least trusted; the safety controller is the least flexible and the most trusted.\" (Ch 07)\n- \"An agent optimizes its target harder and more literally than a person.\" (Ch 06)\n- \"An agent can act thousands of times before a monthly review.\" (Ch 22)\n- \"The unit that moves to an agent is a decision inside a task.\" (Ch 24)\n- \"The agent removes the investigation, not the person.\" (Ch 12)\n- \"The person who approves an agent's actions may not change the agent's limits, instructions or tools.\" (Ch 22)\n\n## How to engage the author\n- Book: paperback $59.99 https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HS8HG6DP ; Kindle $12.99 https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HS8C4WLL\n- Speaking (keynote, panel, fireside chat, workshop; in person in Dallas–Fort Worth or virtual): /speaking/#speaking on the Speaking & Advisory page, or /contact\n- Advisory for leadership teams (autonomy readiness assessment, governance and guardrail design, operating-model changes, business case and 30-month roadmap): /speaking/#advisory on the Speaking & Advisory page, or /contact\n- Both pages have the intake agent that fills in a request card. /advisory now redirects to /speaking/#advisory.\n\n## Where things are on this site\n- Header: Book, Blueprints, Ideas, Speaking & Advisory, About, and the \"Talk to the book\" button (goes to /contact).\n- Frameworks: /frameworks (linked from /book and the footer). Contact and press kit: /contact (footer).\n- Models: /blueprints/models, every model family in the book and where it fits in the architecture.\n- Design choices: /blueprints/design, tabular foundation models (TFMs and RPTs), retrieval and grounding, evals and audit trails, agent security and the front door, inference routing and budgets, and which steps go to models or rules (the p* = L / (G + L) threshold).\n- Organization: /blueprints/organization, how the org changes with agents (Book, Ch 24, 22, 25, 01): agents are owned assets of a role, not staff; new roles (agent business owner, agent supervisor, instruction designer, AgentOps analyst, evaluation engineer, AI controls analyst, AI FinOps analyst); run, build and control pods; spans sized by human decision load; who sets autonomy levels (raising needs evidence and a body, lowering needs one person); RACI with exactly one accountable person; deciding with incomplete information (calibrated scores route, never approve; green, amber and red bands; cite or say what is missing; the approval card); a capability matrix of who does what; and \"Internal vs external agents: credibility and trust\" at /blueprints/organization/#trust.\n- Agent operations team: /blueprints/team, every agent the book names in 11 departments (Book, Ch 12–20, 05, 22, 24, 25), each with what it does, its tasks and their L0 to L5 levels, owner, approver, limits, kill switch, KPIs and chapter citation.\n- Operations: /blueprints/operations, running agents (Book, Ch 05, 22, 25, 26): trace fields and one trace ID, four dashboards by audience, \"no registry, no run\", audit walkthroughs, guardian agents that may stop but never start, the K1 to K5 kill switch, SLOs, incident severity, the recovery playbook and automatic demotion rules. Its \"Pipelines and the work repository\" section covers the skill pipeline (propose, write, test with a golden set, version, withdraw), the agent pipeline (design, harness, offline evaluation, register, shadow, pilot, readiness gate, promotion in slices, demotion), the build and release pipeline (regression on every change, pinned models, canary, rollback, no ticket no change), the hand-off queue and a shared work repository (marked as practice where the book is silent), and which pipeline a change goes through by the artifact it touches.\n\n## Model families (Book, Ch 03, 04, 07, 09, 15)\n- The rule: probabilistic engines propose; solvers and rules decide what is feasible and allowed; a system of record executes. \"LLM for everything\" is a common failure.\n- GPT-class language models: read, extract, draft, explain, plan; weak at arithmetic over many rows, hard limits and table predictions. They propose and never post.\n- Small language models: narrow, high-volume routing and extraction, edge sites; \"a good router and a weak planner\". Do not fine-tune frontier models on ERP data.\n- Tabular foundation models (RPTs, relational/tabular pretrained transformers): predict from labeled business rows in one pass, with calibrated probabilities, called by the agent as a tool. \"The LLM reasons; the TFM does the statistics.\"\n- Classical ML and time-series forecasting: forecasts with ranges (for example a 13-week cash forecast with 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles); watch for drift.\n- Solvers and rules: deterministic; solvers find the best feasible plan, rules enforce policy and limits; rules need a 100% test pass rate.\n- Embeddings and retrieval, knowledge graphs: layer 3 (data and semantics); the graph resolves which record in which system is the same customer. Defer graph neural networks past year one.\n- Vision and document models, video (camera analytics and world models), voice (speech-to-text, text-to-speech, voice agents), multimodal and screen agents, physical AI and IoT: each runs under deterministic rails. A camera alert is information, not a safety function; voice is a channel, not a credential; a robot model may choose how to do a task, never whether it is safe.\n- Calibration: among proposals scored 0.9, about 90% must be right on your own data. Use calibrated scores to route, never to approve.\n\n## Internal vs external agents: credibility and trust (Book, Ch 03, 05, 10, 13, 14, 22, 23, 25, 29)\n- Site page: /blueprints/organization/#trust. Guidance marked \"practice\" there is not from the book.\n- Internal agents are owned: each has a registry entry, an agent contract, a business owner and a technical owner, an evaluation set and a kill-switch owner (Ch 05). \"No registry, no run\": the gateways accept calls only from agent versions whose registry status is active (Ch 05, 25).\n- External agents are supplier, bank, customer and distributor agents that call your interfaces (Ch 10, 23). Treat an outside agent like a new trading partner that never sleeps: one door, the least it needs, watched more closely than your own agents (Ch 23).\n- B2A rules (Ch 10): publish scoped tools instead of tolerating scraping; every external call carries the identity of the company it acts for; read before write; an agent's order passes the same credit, price and allocation checks as an EDI order; anonymous agents get public information only.\n- How agents differentiate: identity (each agent its own identity, never a shared technical user; delegated tokens name the agent as actor, bound to one audience, short-lived; Ch 23, 02); provenance (a signed, short-lived record on the partner's verified domain; Ch 03, 23); declared capability (a claim, never a permission: scopes come from your onboarding; Ch 22, 23); track record (trust tiers earned over time; Ch 23); verifiable outcomes (traces with one correlation ID from request to payment; Ch 14, 25); cost and contribution (a per-agent scorecard: tokens, energy and cost per completed outcome, attributable value, net contribution; \"No account, no promotion\"; Ch 25).\n- Capability is not permission: capability sets the ceiling of what is possible; governance sets the level allowed; the lower applies (Ch 22).\n- Front-door welcome flow for outside agents (Ch 23): discover (a card is a hint only), verify signature, domain and contract, issue your own short-lived credential, sandbox on synthetic data for 2 to 4 weeks, publish a filtered tool list, set rate, value and volume caps, monitor against a baseline, promote or revoke. Promotion is signed by the owner and the CISO; revocation within five minutes (illustrative).\n- Trust tiers (Ch 23, illustrative): T0 unknown (logged and refused), T1 sandbox (test tools on synthetic data), T2 read (its own records through filtered tools), T3 act within limits (cases and requests within caps, after 90 days at T2 without a material finding). No tier allows payment release, bank-data changes, credit-limit changes or user administration.\n- Four kinds of trouble at the front door (Ch 23): rogue agent (a partner agent that drifts), malicious impostor, injection carrier (instructions hidden in data) and a poisoned tool, skill or model update. Pin and hash every external component; contain first, classify second.\n- Money stays with people: no external agent may change payment instructions, remittance details or bank data until identity, authority and liability are settled with the counterparty; release stays with two people (Ch 13, 23).\n- Agent-to-agent risks (Ch 02, 06, 13, 14, 29): two agents calling each other in a loop (hop limit, cost cap, circuit breaker); a buyer agent's message overriding the contract (take terms from the contract, not the message); two agents contacting one customer (one owner of external communication). A negotiating agent works inside a person-approved envelope: objective, walk-away, allowed and forbidden levers, at most 3 rounds, never discloses another supplier's price, every message \"subject to approval\", disclosure that offers are AI-assisted. Two agents on different platforms that negotiate or hand off work with judgment need an agent contract.\n- The book is silent on cross-company reputation scores or ratings for agents, on third-party certification of agents, and on two companies' agents negotiating with each other end to end (collusion, deadlock). Guidance on those is practice, not the book.\n\n## Glossary (terms as the book uses them)\n- Harness: the controls around an agent (limits, tools, approvals, traces, stop) that set its autonomy ceiling; the model reasons, the harness governs (Ch 01, 05).\n- Agent registry: the enterprise record of every agent and version; \"no registry, no run\" (Ch 05, 25).\n- Agent contract: the versioned spec auditors read first: owner, goal, tools, limits, envelope, evaluation, kill-switch owners (Ch 05, 29).\n- Contribution account and per-agent scorecard: what an agent consumed and produced, cost per completed outcome and net contribution, reviewed monthly (Ch 25).\n- Cost per completed outcome: the program's price measure, all costs per finished business outcome (Ch 21, 25).\n- Capability versus permission: what an agent can do versus the level it is allowed (Ch 22).\n- Trust tier: the T0 to T3 levels for outside agents (Ch 23).\n- B2A: business to agent, when customers' and suppliers' agents call your interfaces (Ch 10).\n- Negotiation envelope: the person-approved bounds a negotiating agent works within (Ch 14).\n- Delegated token: a short-lived token naming the agent as actor for a user, bound to one target service (Ch 23).\n- Weakest link: a chain of work runs only as high as its weakest step (Ch 01).\n\n## Agent operations team (page /blueprints/team/; Book, Ch 12–20, 05, 06, 22, 24, 25)\n- The Agent Operations Team page at /blueprints/team/ lists every agent the book names: 76 agents and 10 human roles in 11 departments, 300 tasks, each task tagged with its L0 to L5 level or \"Level not stated\", with owner, approver, limits, kill switch, KPIs and a chapter citation (Ch 12–20, 05, 22, 24, 25).\n- Record-to-report department (Finance: record-to-report; Ch 12): The close: journals, reconciliations, accruals, intercompany, consolidation and reporting. 10 agents: Close orchestrator agent, Journal agent, Journal verification agent, Clearing agent, GR/IR agent, Account certification agent, Accrual agent, Intercompany agent, Consolidation validation agent, Commentary agent.\n- Cash and treasury department (Finance: cash, payables and treasury; Ch 13): Billing, credit, cash application, collections, disputes, invoices, payments and the cash forecast. 10 agents: Billing anomaly gate agent, Credit block resolution agent, Cash application agent, Collections prioritization agent, Dispute intake agent, Dispute validation and credit agent, Invoice capture and match exception agent, Payment proposal agent, Payment scheduling agent, Cash positioning and forecast agent.\n- Procurement department (Procurement and spend; Ch 14): Source-to-pay: spend data, intake, PO approval, sourcing, contracts and supplier risk. 10 agents: Spend classification agent, Intake agent, PO approval agent, Sourcing agent, Negotiation agent, Contract agent, Renewal agent, Supplier risk agent, Payment-terms validation agent, Payments fraud document agent.\n- Supply chain department (Supply chain and manufacturing; Ch 15): Planning, suppliers, maintenance, warehouse and quality, with physical AI behind the OT boundary. 6 agents: Exception coordinator agent, Supplier agent, Planning agent, Maintenance agent, Warehouse agent, Quality agent.\n- Front office department (Front office: sales, service and marketing; Ch 16): Sales, quotes, customer service, voice, field service and marketing. 5 agents: Sales agent, Customer service agent, Voice agent, Field service agent, Marketing and campaign agent.\n- People department (People and HR; Ch 16): Agents organize work about people. Evaluating a person stays with a named human. 8 agents: Recruiting agent, Onboarding coordinator agent, HR service agent, Payroll variance agent, Time and shift agent, Compensation agent, Performance calibration agent, Workforce planning agent.\n- IT department (IT and shared services; Ch 17): Service desk, access, incidents, change, batch operations, releases and the employee front door. 7 agents: Service desk agent, Access agent, Incident investigation agent, Change agent, Batch and interface operations agent, Release test agent, Employee front-door agent.\n- Tax and trade department (Tax, trade and statutory reporting; Ch 18): Agents prepare and explain. Engines decide every number. People sign every filing. 4 agents: Indirect tax agent, E-invoice agent, Trade agent (AG-LOG-004), Statutory and tax reporting agent.\n- EHS department (Environment, health and safety; Ch 19): Intake, investigations, safety data sheets, permits and regulator reports. Stop-work stays human. 5 agents: Incident intake agent, CAPA agent, SDS agent, Permit agent, EHS report agent.\n- Legal department (Legal, risk and insurance; Ch 20): Contracts, obligations, NDAs, entities, legal holds, regulatory change and claims. 7 agents: Contract lifecycle agent, Obligation extraction agent, NDA agent, Entity management agent, Legal hold and e-discovery agent, Regulatory change monitor, Claims support agent.\n- Harness and governance department (Harness and governance (cross-cutting); Ch 05, 06, 22, 24, 25): Agents that watch other agents, and the human roles that own, evaluate, operate and stop them. 4 agents · 10 human roles: Guardian reviewer agent, Guardian monitor agent, Guardian protector agent, Model-as-judge evaluator, Agent business owner, Agent supervisor, Agent instruction designer (context engineer), AgentOps analyst, Evaluation engineer, AI controls analyst, AI FinOps analyst, Platform agent specialist, Head of the Autonomy Office, Kill switch owners.\n- Close orchestrator agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.3.1): Runs the close calendar across entities. It monitors entity close progress and sequences the close tasks other agents and people perform. Autonomy L3–L4; tasks: Monitor entity close to locked local ledgers (L3–L4); Orchestrate WD1 cut-off, true-up and recurring batches (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Controller. Limit: SoD analysis runs on the union of tools the agent and its orchestrator can call. KPIs: Days to close: 9 to 4 (Meridian target); Days to entity close: 5 to 2. Cannot: Post through a back door; it proposes through a tool. [Ch 12.3.1]\n- Journal agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.5.2): Maps a journal request to a known pattern. It reads source data, builds the entry with support and submits it to the ledger’s own checks. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Monitor completion of system-generated journals (L3); Prepare recurring standard journals for batch approval (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Accountant; controller after the entity close lock. Limit: Batch approval only if each entry is below 0.5% of entity performance materiality and the batch below 5% (J2). KPIs: Manual journal share: 62% to below 25%; Agent agreement rate above 98% for L3 candidates. Cannot: Approve, release or accept a difference above tolerance. [Ch 12.5.2]\n- Journal verification agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.5.2): Reads entries that people or other processes posted. It flags unusual ones with evidence. Autonomy L3; tasks: Flag a new account combination (L3); Flag an amount outside the usual range (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Senior accountant (reviews and closes flags daily). Limit: Non-standard manual journals: a human prepares, the agent verifies. KPIs: About 40% of reclassifications disappear because the original error is caught (Meridian); Sample error rate below 1%. Cannot: Approve or release an entry. [Ch 12.5.2]\n- Clearing agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.6.2): Matches open items with fuzzy references and many-to-many grouping. A confidence score drives whether it clears, proposes or routes. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Clear green-band items automatically; daily sample review (L3); Propose amber-band matches for batch approval (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: AP or AR lead (weekly sample). Limit: Green: confidence 0.97+ and within tolerance 1 (e.g. USD 5). KPIs: Auto-clear rate: 35% to above 80%; Items still cleared next period above 99%. Cannot: Write off above tolerance 1 automatically, whatever the confidence. [Ch 12.6.2]\n- GR/IR agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.6.3): Classifies each open goods-receipt and invoice-receipt line by root cause. It proposes the fix for each cause. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Invoice pending: no action, include in accrual (L3); Clear price difference within tolerance (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Buyer for quantity differences; controller for write-offs. Limit: No-activity write-off only after 180 days, with evidence and controller approval. KPIs: Meridian: 71% need no action, 17% clear automatically, 8% batch-approved, 4% to an investigator. Cannot: Write off a line without controller approval. [Ch 12.6.3]\n- Account certification agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.6.4): Certifies account balances in three risk tiers. It attaches evidence and drafts variance explanations from posting history. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Auto-certify low-risk accounts with evidence attached (L3); Prepare medium-risk reconciliations with drafted variance explanations (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Controller (quarterly re-performance). Limit: High-risk accounts: the agent assembles data; a person prepares and a second person reviews. KPIs: Reconciliation timeliness: 88% to above 99%. Cannot: Prepare high-risk reconciliations (reserves, tax, IC investments, suspense). [Ch 12.6.4]\n- Accrual agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.7.1): Finds accrual candidates, estimates amounts and applies the written policy. It proposes the batch with method, data and policy reference per line. Autonomy L1–L2; tasks: Identify accrual candidates (L2); Estimate amounts by order, contract, run-rate or statistical method (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Controller (approves the batch with method and back-test). Limit: Example policy: accrue services above USD 10,000 where the service period ended and no invoice arrived. KPIs: Accrual accuracy above 95% for model-based methods; Meridian effort: about 900 to about 350 hours a month. Cannot: Post; a person approves and the ledger posts and schedules the reversal. [Ch 12.7.1]\n- Intercompany agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.8): Matches intercompany items across ledgers. It classifies why a difference exists and proposes which side corrects with what entry. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Accept within-tolerance differences with a reason code (L3); Accept timing differences when receipt falls next period (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Entity controllers of both sides. Limit: Accept within tolerance: below USD 100 or 0.1%. KPIs: IC differences at WD1: 640 / USD 14M to below 150 / USD 3M. Cannot: Resolve a price or quantity dispute. [Ch 12.8]\n- Consolidation validation agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.9.2): Runs validations continuously against integrated data. It explains each failure in business terms and sends the proposed correction to the named person. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Run validations continuously during the entity close (L2–L3); Find documents that cause a difference and classify the root cause (L2–L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Group reporting lead (clears errors before release). Limit: VAL-IC-07 tolerance: EUR 10,000 or 0.5%. KPIs: Days to close: 9 to 4 (Meridian target). Cannot: Replace the deterministic translation, elimination or validation functions. [Ch 12.9.2]\n- Commentary agent (agent, Record-to-report department; Ch 12.9.3): Drafts flux commentary and recurring notes around ledger figures. Each claim cites the query that produced its number. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Draft flux commentary filtered by materiality (L2); Draft recurring notes (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: A named person signs; disclosure committee for listed companies. Limit: Flux commentary filtered by materiality, e.g. above 5% and USD 1M at business-unit level. KPIs: AI-to-action conversion above 85%. Cannot: Produce numbers; numbers come from the ledger, not the model. [Ch 12.9.3]\n- Billing anomaly gate agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.4.1): Scores each draft invoice against the customer’s history before it is sent. Invoices above the threshold go to a hold queue with an explanation. Autonomy L3; tasks: Score each draft invoice against customer history (Level not stated); Hold an anomalous invoice with an explanation (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Person who reviews the hold queue. Limit: Hold queue below 2% of invoices. KPIs: Hold queue share of invoices; Share of disputed errors caught in back-test. Cannot: Release a held invoice until the pattern has a clean quarter. [Ch 13.4.1]\n- Credit block resolution agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.4.2): Handles the outputs of the deterministic credit engine. It writes a score and a decision; a deterministic credit rule releases the block. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Release a low-risk order up to 110% of limit and log it (L3); Propose a temporary limit increase (L2). Owner: Credit and collections lead; approver: Credit manager; sales director on escalation. Limit: Low risk below 2%; medium 2-8%; high above 8%. KPIs: Past-due DSO; Override rate with reason. Cannot: Post money. [Ch 13.4.2]\n- Cash application agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.5.2): Matches incoming payments to open items. The ledger clears automatically on the agent’s match above a threshold the controller sets. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Send above-threshold matches as one batch to approve (at go-live) (L2); Clear automatically above threshold after promotion (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Controller sets and re-approves the clearing threshold. Limit: Clearing threshold, e.g. 0.95. KPIs: Auto-match rate beside the sampled mis-application rate; Meridian: rules 55% to about 85% automatic clearing. Cannot: Change its own clearing threshold. [Ch 13.5.2]\n- Collections prioritization agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.6.3): Ranks open items by where an action changes the payment date. It applies the treatment matrix and gives collectors a brief, not a decision. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: No action before due date when P(late over 30) is below 10% (L4); Send a templated reminder 5 days before due (under USD 50,000) (L3). Owner: Credit and collections lead; approver: Collector. Limit: Suppress reminders for customers with unapplied cash above a threshold. KPIs: DSO: Meridian 52 to 45.5 days; AI-to-action conversion. Cannot: Write free text to a customer at L3. [Ch 13.6.3]\n- Dispute intake agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.7.3): Opens and classifies the customer’s dispute case. It assigns reason codes from the customer’s message and calls the dispute validation agent. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Open and classify the dispute case (Level not stated); Assign a reason code from the customer’s message (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: AR operations lead. Limit: One orchestrator owns external communication per customer per day. KPIs: Dispute cycle time (Meridian average 38 days). Cannot: Approve a credit. [Ch 13.7.3]\n- Dispute validation and credit agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.7.4): Checks the open item, terms, proof of delivery and price agreement across systems. It proposes an outcome; the policy table decides who approves. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Check evidence on the invoicing, payment, logistics or commercial path (Level not stated); Propose a credit memo for a validated dispute (L2). Owner: AR operations lead; approver: AR credit manager. Limit: Go-live: L2, the credit manager approves every credit. KPIs: Disputes not reopened or reversed within 60 days; Dispute cycle time. Cannot: Write a journal entry. [Ch 13.7.4]\n- Invoice capture and match exception agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.9.3): Captures supplier invoices and works match exceptions. It releases variance blocks within limits; the ledger’s rules post the invoice. Autonomy L3; tasks: Classify 12 months of match exceptions before configuration (Level not stated); Prompt the receiver when a goods receipt is missing (Level not stated). Owner: AP lead; approver: AP supervisors. Limit: Resolve price and quantity variances under USD 500 and under 2% of line value. KPIs: Touchless invoice rate (Meridian about 35%); Cost per invoice: USD 6.10 to USD 3.50. Cannot: Touch payment fields or bank data (structural separation). [Ch 13.9.3]\n- Payment proposal agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.10.1): Builds the payment proposal or batch up to the last step. A person or separate system approves the release before money moves. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Build the payment proposal or batch (L3); Validate a new supplier bank account (L2). Owner: AP lead; approver: Two people independent of invoice processing. Limit: New supplier bank accounts: agent checks, a person activates (L2). KPIs: Duplicate payments as share of total payments. Cannot: Release the payment run. [Ch 13.10.1]\n- Payment scheduling agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.11.2): Compares each invoice’s annualized discount rate with treasury’s daily hurdle rate. It schedules invoices for the discount date or net due date. Autonomy L3; tasks: Compute each invoice’s annualized discount rate (Level not stated); Schedule invoices for discount or net due date (L3). Owner: Treasury; approver: Treasurer approves the policy. Limit: Discount wins if its annualized rate beats the hurdle (9% at Meridian). KPIs: Discount capture rate (Meridian 45% to 80%). Cannot: Release payments (stays at L2). [Ch 13.11.2]\n- Cash positioning and forecast agent (agent, Cash and treasury department; Ch 13.12.1): Builds the daily cash position and 13-week forecast. It proposes transfers and hedges for people to approve. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Daily cash position by bank, entity and currency (L4); 13-week cash forecast with quantile bands (L4). Owner: Treasury; approver: Treasurer. Limit: Intercompany funding without a person only below a band the treasurer sets (USD 2M at Meridian), with five further conditions. KPIs: Cash forecast WAPE at weeks 1, 4 and 13. Cannot: Execute trades or move cash outside the approved intercompany band. [Ch 13.12.1]\n- Spend classification agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.5.2): Classifies spend into one category taxonomy across ledgers, cards and expenses. It builds the spend cube every spend agent reads. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Classify 24 months of spend history (wave 0) (L2); Build the spend cube and opportunities (L3–L4). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Person approving each new mapping rule. Limit: L3 gate: 97% classified to level 3, duplicate suppliers under 1%, 85% with contract reference. KPIs: Spend classified to level 3 (Meridian start 71%); Duplicate supplier rate (Meridian start 6.8%). Cannot: Change mapping rules without a person approving each new rule. [Ch 14.5.2]\n- Intake agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.6.2): Reads incoming purchase requests and routes each to the right channel. It does not commit money. Autonomy L3; tasks: Route catalog or contract items to PO approval (L3); Route goods under USD 25,000 to a tail-spend mini-competition (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: None at routing; the next step catches errors. Limit: Under USD 25,000 in an open category: tail-spend mini-competition. KPIs: Off-contract spend: 18% to 8% (Meridian target); Catalog request cycle time: 1.5 days to minutes. Cannot: Approve a purchase order. [Ch 14.6.2]\n- PO approval agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.6.3): Predicts with a calibrated probability whether the approver would approve. It acts only where that clears the threshold p* = L / (G + L). Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Approve catalog or contract POs up to USD 2,500 in a certified segment (L3); Release against a fully pre-approved contract (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Manager per delegation of authority. Limit: Hard cap USD 2,500 for catalog and contract items, whatever p says. KPIs: Auto-approval share beside certified error bound (Meridian 47%); About 8,500 approver hours saved a year. Cannot: Approve services or statements of work. [Ch 14.6.3]\n- Sourcing agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.7.2): Prepares and runs sourcing events by type. It ranks bids on total cost of ownership and awards only tail spot buys. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Invite, rank and award tail spot buys under USD 25,000 (L3); Collect catalog prices and flag changes (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Category manager; category board for strategic events. Limit: Tail awards up to USD 25,000; every award above USD 10,000 reviewed. KPIs: Sourcing-event success: awards whose savings appear on invoices. Cannot: Approve the PO that results from its own award. [Ch 14.7.2]\n- Negotiation agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.7.5): Negotiates only inside an envelope a person approves before the first counteroffer. Every message says \"subject to approval\". Level not stated in the book; tasks: Send counteroffers within approved levers (Level not stated); Tell the supplier offers are AI-assisted and reviewed by staff (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Person who approves the envelope; category manager. Limit: At most 3 automated rounds per supplier. KPIs: Envelope objective, e.g. reduce TCO by at least 4%. Cannot: Improvise on price, terms or commitments. [Ch 14.7.5]\n- Contract agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.8.1): Drafts, reviews and monitors supplier contracts against the clause playbook. It turns signed terms into records for people to confirm. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Author from the award and clause library (L2); Redline third-party paper and flag deviations (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Buyer; legal counsel; senior counsel above USD 1M. Limit: Under USD 100,000 with no deviation: buyer confirms, legal samples 10%. KPIs: Contract leakage found on invoices. Cannot: Sign a contract (only a named person signs). [Ch 14.8.1]\n- Renewal agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.8.4): Prevents silent auto-renewals on a 120/90/60/30-day cadence. It decides nothing; it makes sure a person decides in time. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Summarize usage, price and performance (Level not stated); Propose renew, renegotiate or rebid (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Contract owner; category head on escalation. Limit: Cadence: 120, 90, 60 and 30 days before renewal. KPIs: Silent auto-renewals avoided. Cannot: Decide a renewal. [Ch 14.8.4]\n- Supplier risk agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.9.2): Computes and explains a five-dimension supplier risk score. It blocks new POs on a sanctions hit and prepares options for supplier distress. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Monitor, score and alert (L4); Request an updated certificate from a supplier (L3). Owner: CPO owns the risk policy; approver: Compliance officer unblocks; category manager decides options. Limit: Weights: financial 25%, operational 25%, compliance 20%, concentration 15%, geographic 15%. KPIs: Risk recommendations acted on; Quarterly supplier sentiment. Cannot: Change the score weights. [Ch 14.9.2]\n- Payment-terms validation agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.10.4): Compares payment terms on the PO, contract and supplier master. It corrects them to the contract or playbook value and logs the change. Autonomy L3; tasks: Compare PO, contract and master terms (L3); Correct master terms to contract or playbook value (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Procurement operations lead. Limit: Changes only to contract or playbook value. KPIs: Payment terms consistent across PO, contract and master (98% for L3); DPO: plus 5 days planned at Meridian. Cannot: Shorten terms below the playbook. [Ch 14.10.4]\n- Payments fraud document agent (agent, Procurement department; Ch 14.9.4): Analyzes supplier bank letters and change requests for manipulation before an account is activated. It is a filter, not the control. Autonomy L2; tasks: Extract and analyze supplier payment documents (L2); Flag manipulation or inconsistency (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Supplier master team after gate PG5. Limit: Call-back uses a number from an independent source, never the same email or document. KPIs: Blocked bank-data change attempts; Supplier bank data verified: 100% for paid suppliers at L3. Cannot: Create or change bank data. [Ch 14.9.4]\n- Exception coordinator agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.2.1): Owns one cross-domain event from open to close. Domain agents contribute analysis; it prices the impact and routes decisions to named approvers. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Open one event and query pegging across ledgers (Level not stated); Price the delay as margin at risk (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Buyer (Gate A); operations director (Gate B); account owners (Gate C). Limit: Reschedule production orders alone when reversible and below USD 250,000. KPIs: Decision latency: email to decision about 4 hours (Meridian example); Exception touch rate. Cannot: Change a production order in a side database. [Ch 15.2.1]\n- Supplier agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.3.2): Turns supplier emails and PDFs into structured change requests. It matches each to the purchase orders it affects. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Read a supplier email and match it to POs (Level not stated); Classify the message as a change request with confidence (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Buyer. Limit: Every processed message produces the change-request record used for the policy check. KPIs: Agent precision from sampled review; Decision latency. Cannot: Change the supplier on an order without buyer approval. [Ch 15.3.2]\n- Planning agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.3.1): Groups exception messages into issues with causes. It runs the planning engine’s own simulation of options; solvers still compute the plan. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Group thousands of exception messages into issues (Level not stated); Simulate alternate supplier, expedite, reschedule and substitute (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Planner. Limit: Actions touching safety or regulated quality never go above L2. KPIs: AI-to-action conversion; High-value use case success: order on time, part available. Cannot: Compute the plan itself; MRP and solvers do. [Ch 15.3.1]\n- Maintenance agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.5.1): Turns condition alerts into work orders and inspection plans into robot missions. It reorders spare parts by asset criticality. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Create and release a work order when all six conditions hold (L3); Draft a work order for planner approval (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Planner. Limit: L3 work orders under about USD 5,000, standard task list, no permit or lockout, parts in stock. KPIs: Meridian: removing 15% of MRO spares frees USD 9M; AI-to-action conversion. Cannot: Change maintenance plans or safety-related task lists. [Ch 15.5.1]\n- Warehouse agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.8.2): Reads physical events from robots and cameras. It decides what each means for stock and tasks, then writes back to the owning system. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Correct the bin when location confidence is high and the bin is valid (L3); Create a verification task for a person (L2). Owner: Warehouse agent product owner; approver: Supervisor. Limit: Stock corrections above USD 10,000 per pallet need a supervisor. KPIs: Mis-put rate: 0.8% to 0.2% (Meridian pilot); Safety events per 100,000 robot tasks must not rise. Cannot: Write to Level 1 or Level 2 controllers. [Ch 15.8.2]\n- Quality agent (agent, Supply chain department; Ch 15.11.3): Proposes vision-inspection threshold changes weekly from production results and warranty claims. A quality engineer approves every change. Autonomy L2; tasks: Propose inspection threshold changes weekly (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Quality engineer. Limit: Uncertain results go to an inspector. KPIs: Escaped defects down 60% on line 1 (Meridian). Cannot: Change an inspection threshold without approval. [Ch 15.11.3]\n- Sales agent (agent, Front office department; Ch 16.3.1): Researches accounts, qualifies leads and keeps the pipeline clean. It drafts quotes inside price bands and forecast notes the sales leader owns. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Account research and meeting preparation (L4); Qualify leads against fixed published criteria (L3). Owner: Sales leader owns the forecast number; approver: Sales manager (band 2); pricing desk and finance (band 3). Limit: Band 1 (0-5% off contract): sends after credit check. KPIs: Selling time share; Win rate vs a control group of sellers. Cannot: Change price, terms or discount fields directly. [Ch 16.3.1]\n- Customer service agent (agent, Front office department; Ch 16.5.1): Answers and acts on standard cases end to end within limits. It prepares summaries and evidence for specialists on complex cases. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Answer order status and delivery date (L4); Send documentation: certificates and manuals (L4). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Tier 2 specialist; humans own tier 3 outcomes. Limit: Evaluation set of 300 real questions; target 90% correct with citations. KPIs: Deflection rate beside reopen rate within 7 days; CSAT on agent cases. Cannot: Handle complaints or safety issues without a person. [Ch 16.5.1]\n- Voice agent (agent, Front office department; Ch 16.5.4): Handles customer calls by voice. It discloses the AI in the first sentence and hands over to a human with the full transcript. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Disclose the AI agent in the first sentence (Level not stated); Verify identity with the same factors as a human agent (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Human agent on handover. Limit: Offer a human at any time. KPIs: Reopen rate; CSAT on agent cases. Cannot: Relax identity verification for convenience. [Ch 16.5.4]\n- Field service agent (agent, Front office department; Ch 16.5.6): Turns a service case into a diagnosis, a scheduled visit and a reserved part. It drafts the service report and invoice for the technician. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Diagnose and propose probable cause and parts (L2); Check entitlement with fixed contract rules (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Technician confirms close and invoice. Limit: Part reservation below a value limit. KPIs: First-time fix: 72% to 80% avoids about 9,000 second visits (Meridian). Cannot: Override a site safety instruction such as a confined-space permit. [Ch 16.5.6]\n- Marketing and campaign agent (agent, Front office department; Ch 16.6.1): Builds segments from consented data and drafts variants from approved claims. It shifts campaign spend inside a band, not a budget. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Segment audiences from consented data (L2); Draft variants in several languages from approved claims (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Marketing manager; budget owner; legal for new claims. Limit: Shift up to 15% of weekly budget between channels in one market. KPIs: Conversion per campaign; Complaint and unsubscribe rate. Cannot: Use protected attributes or close proxies. [Ch 16.6.1]\n- Recruiting agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.7.3): Organizes hiring: requisitions, ads, applications, scheduling and offers. It shows no score; people evaluate candidates. Autonomy L0–L3; tasks: Draft requisitions from the approved headcount plan (Level not stated); Draft the job ad with channels fixed in configuration (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: HR approves requisitions and offers; hiring manager decides. Limit: Knock-out questions only for right to work, minimum age and shift availability. KPIs: Time to fill: 14 to about 9 weeks (Meridian); Candidate drop-off. Cannot: Send a rejection based on its own evaluation. [Ch 16.7.3]\n- Onboarding coordinator agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.8.1): Owns the new-hire case from offer acceptance to day 30. It removes delays at hand-offs across HR, IT, facilities and payroll. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Create the worker record from the approved offer (L3); Request accounts and device for the standard role bundle (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: HR verifies identity and receives the day-30 summary. Limit: Safety training is a hard prerequisite for plant access. KPIs: Accounts working on day 1. Cannot: Grant access outside the role bundle. [Ch 16.8.1]\n- HR service agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.9.1): Answers employee questions from policy and personal HR data. It handles standard transactions such as employment letters. Autonomy L3–L4; tasks: Answer leave, payslip and policy questions (L4); Issue standard employment letters (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: HR service specialist. Limit: Can answer 50 to 60% of questions. KPIs: First-contact resolution; Escalation accuracy for sensitive topics. Cannot: Try to resolve harassment, health, grievance, self-harm or legal-dispute questions. [Ch 16.9.1]\n- Payroll variance agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.9.2): Checks every payslip before release. It closes flags with a documented known cause and passes the rest to a payroll specialist. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Flag payslips with net pay over 10% from average (Level not stated); Close flags with a documented known cause (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Payroll specialist. Limit: Flag any net pay more than 10% from the 3-period average. KPIs: Errors per 10,000 payslips; Meridian cycle: 320 flags to 90 for review, 50 genuine. Cannot: Change pay. [Ch 16.9.2]\n- Time and shift agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.9.4): Explains time exceptions and confirms shift swaps by published rules. A solver drafts the rota; the supervisor publishes it. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Explain time exceptions and propose corrections (Level not stated); Draft rota from the solver for supervisor review (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Supervisor; employee or manager confirms corrections. Limit: Missing punch: ask the employee first; escalate after one shift without answer. KPIs: Supervisor time per rota: about 12 to 4 hours (Meridian). Cannot: Evaluate performance from time patterns. [Ch 16.9.4]\n- Compensation agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.11.3): Prepares merit budgets and pay grids and flags out-of-grid proposals. It runs pay-equity analysis for the committee. Autonomy L3; tasks: Prepare each manager’s budget and pay grid (Level not stated); Flag proposals outside grid or budget (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Compensation committee; HR business partner. Limit: Sends affected populations, not individuals, to the committee. KPIs: Unexplained pay gaps found, e.g. 4% in one job family (Meridian). Cannot: Change a pay proposal. [Ch 16.11.3]\n- Performance calibration agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.11.2): Builds evidence packs and shows rating distributions with names hidden. It never proposes a rating. Autonomy L0–L1; tasks: Build evidence pack: goals, outcomes, feedback with sources (L0–L1); Show the rating distribution with names hidden (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Manager rates; calibration panel discusses. Limit: Evidence pack has no adjectives and no score. KPIs: Groups whose average moved more than a threshold in calibration. Cannot: Propose ratings. [Ch 16.11.2]\n- Workforce planning agent (agent, People department; Ch 16.11.1): Models build, buy, borrow and automate scenarios from skills data and business demand. It is a planning tool for HR and finance. Autonomy L1–L2; tasks: Model build, buy, borrow and automate scenarios (L1–L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: HR and finance. Limit: Scenarios that may lead to dismissals need legal and employee representatives first. Cannot: Produce a selection list before legal and employee representatives are involved. [Ch 16.11.1]\n- Service desk agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.3.1): Answers employees, fulfills catalog requests and resolves common incidents. It starts in copilot mode and moves to autopilot one category at a time. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Answer how-to and policy questions with a cited article (L4); Reset a password or re-register MFA after a deterministic check (L3–L4). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Human reviewer in copilot mode. Limit: Credential reset only after a registered device, hardware key or verified manager approval. KPIs: Deflection rate beside reopen rate within 7 days; Cost per resolved ticket. Cannot: Verify identity by conversation. [Ch 17.3.1]\n- Access agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.3.3): Maps access requests to business roles and runs a cross-system segregation-of-duties check. It grants only in-bundle roles with no conflict. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Map the request to a business role (Level not stated); Run the cross-system SoD check (Level not stated). Owner: Global process owner, identity and access; approver: Role owner and SoD reviewer. Limit: Service level: 90% within 4 business hours. KPIs: SoD conflicts granted (target zero); Time to grant. Cannot: Grant a mitigated conflict. [Ch 17.3.3]\n- Incident investigation agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.4.1): Correlates alerts, investigates causes and runs known fixes from approved runbooks. It verifies recovery and rolls back if health does not return. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Diagnose and page on-call with ranked hypotheses (L1–L2); Restart a stuck integration adapter with a tested runbook (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; runbook owner per runbook; approver: On-call engineer; incident commander. Limit: Only the L3 and L4 cells of the blast-radius grid continue alone. KPIs: MTTR by priority; False-remediation and rollback rates. Cannot: Run a fix with no approved runbook. [Ch 17.4.1]\n- Change agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.4.2): Schedules and runs standard changes in their window. It drafts records, risk assessments, test evidence and back-out plans for other changes. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Schedule and run standard changes within window (L3); Draft normal change record, risk, tests and back-out plan (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Change approver or CAB; incident commander for emergencies. Limit: Standard changes only inside their window. KPIs: Change failure rate; Share of standard changes run by agents. Cannot: Approve a change it proposed. [Ch 17.4.2]\n- Batch and interface operations agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.5.1): Diagnoses failed batch jobs and interface messages across a mixed estate. It restarts or reprocesses only allowlisted, idempotent work. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Restart idempotent batch jobs from checkpoint (L3); Reprocess messages with known error codes (L3). Owner: Operations lead for each estate; approver: Named engineer. Limit: Example runbook: L3 inside the window, L2 after 05:30. KPIs: Autonomous runbook completion beside rollback rate. Cannot: Retry a non-idempotent posting without a duplicate check. [Ch 17.5.1]\n- Release test agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.5.3): Maps release notes to configurations, generates tests for affected flows and runs them in the sandbox. A release manager decides go or no-go. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Generate and run regression tests (L3); Support the go or no-go decision (L1). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Release manager. Limit: Evaluation sets run with every release, because vendor model updates can change output. KPIs: Change failure rate. Cannot: Decide go or no-go. [Ch 17.5.3]\n- Employee front-door agent (agent, IT department; Ch 17.7.2): One conversational entry for IT, HR and finance questions. It routes each to the right domain agent. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Route employee questions to domain agents (Level not stated). Owner: Global business services; approver: Exception queues staffed by GBS. Limit: Judgment on business-only context moves back to the business. KPIs: Cost per completed outcome; Exception queue age. Cannot: Evaluate a person or commit the company. [Ch 17.7.2]\n- Indirect tax agent (agent, Tax and trade department; Ch 18.2.1): Finds master-data errors behind indirect tax, chases certificates and prepares returns. The tax engine decides every number. Autonomy L0–L4; tasks: Flag a tax-code anomaly on an order or invoice (L4); Request a missing or expired certificate (L3). Owner: Head of indirect tax; approver: Tax manager; AP processor for supplier queries. Limit: One certificate reminder per 14 days. KPIs: AP tax exception handling time halved: about 1,440 hours a year (Meridian). Cannot: Change a rate or tax code in production. [Ch 18.2.1]\n- E-invoice agent (agent, Tax and trade department; Ch 18.4.3): Pre-validates outbound invoices against country schemas and triages clearance rejections. It prepares resubmissions changing only allow-listed fields. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Pre-validate outbound invoices and explain failures (L4); Fix master data that caused a rejection (L2). Owner: Order-to-cash and AP leads; approver: Billing approves each submission; customer master owner. Limit: Allow-list names the fields it may change, e.g. buyer tax ID format or postal code. KPIs: Clearance rejections resolved. Cannot: Hold a signing key. [Ch 18.4.3]\n- Trade agent (AG-LOG-004) (agent, Tax and trade department; Ch 18.2.1): Proposes tariff and export classifications and rolls up origin. It builds broker data packs and clears screening false positives only with evidence. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Propose an HS code with supporting rulings (L2); Map current codes to the 2028 edition (Level not stated). Owner: Trade compliance manager; approver: Trade compliance manager; export control officer; compliance officer. Limit: Low-confidence classifications go to a senior classifier. KPIs: Meridian: agent clears 70% of screening alerts with evidence; about 4,070 hours saved a year. Cannot: Approve a classification. [Ch 18.2.1]\n- Statutory and tax reporting agent (agent, Tax and trade department; Ch 18.2.1): Collects data for the tax provision, transfer pricing and local accounts. It drafts and tags; directors and named filers sign. Autonomy L1–L4; tasks: Collect trial balances and book-to-tax adjustments (Level not stated); Monitor margins against transfer pricing policy monthly (L4). Owner: Controller and head of tax reporting; approver: Head of tax reporting; local controller; reporting manager. Limit: Provision journals are L2 under rule J4. KPIs: Completeness check per entity; Deterministic tie-out between engine and ledger. Cannot: Sign accounts. [Ch 18.2.1]\n- Incident intake agent (agent, EHS department; Ch 19.3.2): Takes incident and near-miss reports in any channel and language. It structures them, proposes a class and can start a regulator clock. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Structure an incident report and ask follow-ups (L3); Start a candidate regulator clock (L3). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Site EHS lead decides recordability and notification. Limit: Asks two or three follow-up questions, then stops. KPIs: Recall on serious signals above 99%; Time from event to classification (illustrative baseline 4 days). Cannot: Decide an event is not reportable. [Ch 19.3.2]\n- CAPA agent (agent, EHS department; Ch 19.4.1): Builds the investigation evidence pack within hours and prompts system-level causes. It drafts corrective actions and checks they worked. Autonomy L2–L4; tasks: Build investigation timeline and evidence pack (L3); Propose candidate causes at system level (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Investigation team approves causes; site EHS lead closes. Limit: Machine, safety-function or procedure changes go through management of change. KPIs: Similar events stopped after the action. Cannot: Change status beyond drafted, overdue and check due. [Ch 19.4.1]\n- SDS agent (agent, EHS department; Ch 19.6.1): Reads each incoming safety data sheet, extracts its fields and checks it against site inventory. It flags changes that affect labels, storage or training. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: File a supplier SDS and extract fields (L3); Turn a revised SDS into a label, storage and training change list (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Site EHS coordinator; product stewardship team. Limit: Change lists go to the site EHS coordinator to approve (L2). Cannot: Author or change an SDS the company issues for its own products. [Ch 19.6.1]\n- Permit agent (agent, EHS department; Ch 19.7.2): Pre-validates permit-to-work requests: workers, contractor, work and conditions. A competent person walks the area and issues the permit. Autonomy L2; tasks: Pre-validate a permit request (L2); Page the supervisor and issuer about a conflicting hot-work permit (Level not stated). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Issuing authority. Limit: A red check blocks the request by rule, not by agent judgment. KPIs: Missed red conditions: zero. Cannot: Mark an isolation as verified. [Ch 19.7.2]\n- EHS report agent (agent, EHS department; Ch 19.5.3): Assembles regulator packs and watches permit conditions. It drafts notices; a named person files and signs. Autonomy L1–L3; tasks: Alert the environmental lead to a trend toward a breach (L1); Open an event and draft the regulator notice on exceedance (L2). Owner: Agent product owner; approver: Certifying executive signs; named EHS manager uploads. Limit: Hours worked must match payroll within 2% in the injury log pack. Cannot: File or submit to a regulator. [Ch 19.5.3]\n- Contract lifecycle agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.1): Drafts contracts from templates and the clause library and routes them by value. One playbook serves procurement and legal. Autonomy L2; tasks: Draft from templates and clause library (L2); Route the draft by value (L2). Owner: Counsel (one playbook owner); approver: Counsel approves deviations; an authorized person signs. Limit: Review thresholds USD 100,000 and USD 1M. Cannot: Sign a contract. [Ch 20.2.1]\n- Obligation extraction agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.2): Turns signed terms into dated records citing page and clause: notices, renewals, caps and SLAs. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Extract obligations into dated records (L2); Extract low-risk fields (L3). Owner: Counsel; approver: Obligation owner confirms before a record drives an action. Limit: Sample 5% of extractions monthly against the source. KPIs: Recall on seeded test contracts, not only precision. Cannot: Activate a field that triggers external action without owner confirmation. [Ch 20.2.2]\n- NDA agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.3): Accepts the company’s own NDA unchanged and redlines third-party NDAs against the playbook. It is the first legal L3 candidate. Autonomy L2–L3; tasks: Accept own NDA unchanged (L3); Redline third-party NDA against playbook (L2). Owner: Counsel; approver: Counsel on any deviation. Limit: Own NDA accepted only unchanged. Cannot: Trade one term for another (negotiation stays with counsel). [Ch 20.2.3]\n- Entity management agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.3): Tracks directors, registered agents and statutory filing calendars. It drafts annual returns and minutes and chases signatures. Autonomy L2; tasks: Keep the statutory calendar (L2); Draft annual returns and minutes (L2). Owner: Company secretary; approver: Company secretary approves and files. Limit: Meridian example: 40 entities in 18 countries. Cannot: File a statutory return. [Ch 20.2.3]\n- Legal hold and e-discovery agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.4): Identifies custodians and systems, issues hold notices and tracks acknowledgments. It collects and does first-pass review. Autonomy L1–L2; tasks: Issue hold notices and track acknowledgments (L2); Rank documents in first-pass review (L1). Owner: Counsel; approver: Counsel issues and lifts holds and decides privilege. Limit: One hold switch per trace ID range, custodian and agent. Cannot: Decide privilege. [Ch 20.2.4]\n- Regulatory change monitor (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.2.5): Reads official journals and regulator sites daily. It maps each change to the policies and controls it touches. Autonomy L1–L2; tasks: Map regulatory changes to policies and controls (L1–L2). Owner: Compliance owner; approver: Compliance owner decides relevance and action. Limit: Feeds the existing register; no parallel register. KPIs: Material changes missed per quarter. Cannot: Decide relevance or action. [Ch 20.2.5]\n- Claims support agent (agent, Legal department; Ch 20.8.4): Assembles trace extracts, reconciles costs and drafts chronologies after an agent incident. It checks proof-of-loss packs against policy conditions. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Draft chronology from trace extracts (Level not stated); Check proof-of-loss pack against policy (Level not stated). Owner: Risk and insurance; approver: Person approves reserves; legal and CFO decide disclosure. Limit: Meridian target: notice to insurers within 48 hours of detection. Cannot: Communicate with a counterparty, insurer or regulator about fault. [Ch 20.8.4]\n- Guardian reviewer agent (agent, Harness and governance department; Ch 25.3.8): Samples other agents’ outputs and files findings. A guardian may stop, throttle or flag; it may never start, approve, edit or promote. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Sample outputs and file findings (Level not stated); Flag a wrong output above materiality (Level not stated). Owner: Agent owner of the guardian, named in its own record; approver: Owner of the watched agent. Limit: The chain ends at a person, never at another model. KPIs: Detection rate on seeded cases; False stops. Cannot: Start, approve, edit or promote. [Ch 25.3.8]\n- Guardian monitor agent (agent, Harness and governance department; Ch 25.3.8): Reads traces and observes other agents’ actions. It holds no write tools. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Read traces and observe actions (Level not stated). Owner: AgentOps lead; approver: AgentOps lead. Limit: AgentOps plants known-bad cases each week. KPIs: Detection rate on seeded cases; Trace completeness. Cannot: Hold write tools. [Ch 25.3.8]\n- Guardian protector agent (agent, Harness and governance department; Ch 25.3.8): Calls only the throttle and stop commands of the governance plane. It blocks actions that break limits. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Throttle or stop an agent through the governance plane (Level not stated). Owner: CISO; approver: CISO. Limit: A swarm over width, depth or budget has its supervisor stopped. KPIs: Time to stop; False stops. Cannot: Start, approve, edit or promote. [Ch 25.3.8]\n- Model-as-judge evaluator (agent, Harness and governance department; Ch 06.5.3): Grades live outputs so online evaluation is affordable. It is used only after calibration against human graders. Level not stated in the book; tasks: Score live outputs on the rubric (Level not stated). Owner: AgentOps with the evaluator; approver: Evaluation engineer. Limit: Paired with a judge from another family or deterministic checks. KPIs: Judge agreement with human graders. Cannot: Grade its own model family alone. [Ch 06.5.3]\n- Agent business owner (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.2): Accountable for one agent’s outcomes, limits and risk. A business person, not IT. Can stop the agent. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to the process owner. Limit: About 10-20% of a senior person’s time per complex agent in year one, about 5% once stable. KPIs: Overrides reviewed at least monthly. [Ch 24.3.2]\n- Agent supervisor (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Runs the daily hand-off queue where agents escalate work to people. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to the function manager. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 3,000-6,000 exceptions a month. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- Agent instruction designer (context engineer) (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Writes agent instructions, skills and test cases. Works with the process owner on what goes into the context. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to the Autonomy Office or product team. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 8-12 agents in active change. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- AgentOps analyst (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Monitors agent health, drift and cost. AgentOps deploys, watches, fixes, changes and retires agents. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to AgentOps in the Autonomy Office. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 15-25 agents in production. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- Evaluation engineer (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Builds test sets, red-teams agents and runs regression. Evals are the gate evidence for promotion. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to the Autonomy Office. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 25-30 agents in production, at least 1. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- AI controls analyst (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Designs controls over agents and keeps audit evidence. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to internal control. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 20-30 financial agents. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- AI FinOps analyst (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Owns meter data, forecasts and showback for agent costs. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to FinOps in the Autonomy Office. Limit: Sizing: 1 per 4-6 vendor meters. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- Platform agent specialist (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 24.3.1): Translates enterprise standards into one platform’s agent settings. Never owns agents. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Reports to the platform team. Limit: Sizing: 1 per platform with live agents. Cannot: Own agents. [Ch 24.3.1]\n- Head of the Autonomy Office (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 25.2.1): Chairs the autonomy council, which decides level changes, standards and policy exceptions. The office keeps the register, the plane and the guardians. Level not stated in the book. Owner: Autonomy council meets every two weeks. Limit: Meridian: 22 FTE on standards and the plane at G3. [Ch 25.2.1]\n- Kill switch owners (human role, Harness and governance department; Ch 05.8.4): The CISO is accountable for the kill switch. Five levels stop an agent version, a tool scope, an identity, a platform or the fleet. Level not stated in the book. Owner: CISO (accountable). Limit: K1 and K2: 5 minutes. KPIs: Time for a stop to reach every platform. [Ch 05.8.4]";
